# D31's acceptance scenario: hand-written against testFlow-tests' perf-0 contended checkout
# endpoint (POST /orders, real Postgres row-lock serialization on one hot product's stock row).
# Every VU looks up the hot product then checks out one unit of it — the product lookup is a
# fast, uncontended request; the checkout is the contended one. tflw's `scenario` has no
# once-per-run setup phase (unlike k6's `setup()`), so both sides of this comparison resolve the
# product id every iteration for a fair, apples-to-apples comparison — see acceptance/README.md's
# perf leg for that finding written up in full.
#
# Run `POST /admin/load/reset` (bearer admin auth) against http://localhost:4001 before each run
# for a clean baseline (fresh stock, no leftover orders from a prior run).

scenario "checkout burst on the contended product" as load
  ramp to 60 users over 20s
  api GET /products?q=Load%20Test%20Widget
  expect status equals 200
  capture body[0].id as productId
  api POST /orders body { items: [{ productId: {productId}, quantity: 1 }] }
  expect status equals 201
  threshold error rate is less than 1%
  threshold p95 duration is less than 250ms
